        "narrative": "Allocation is now the whole story. DRAM and HBM capacity for 2027 is described as sold out across Micron, Samsung and SK Hynix, NAND is sold out on current annual capacity, and customers are being allocated only 60-70% of the volumes they requested, with Kioxia and SK Hynix finalising the rest by August. The framing in the feed is that 2027 is the most severe point of the shortage.\n\nSandisk's fiscal fourth quarter turned that industry narrative into a company number: revenue of $8.97bn, adjusted EPS of $39.25, gross margin near 78%, first revenue recognised from the QLC-based Stargate platform, and a fiscal Q1 2027 guide of $10.3-10.8bn with $44-46 of EPS. That is the NAND-specific pricing evidence the desk had been holding out for.\n\nWhat the theme cannot tell you is where in the cycle this sits. The shares trade at a single-digit multiple of guided earnings, which is the market pricing a peak the guidance does not show. He remains explicitly bullish on memory while noting the sector's attention has rotated to photonics \u2014 his own framing of a market that moves from bottleneck to bottleneck.",
