        "category": "hyperscalers",
        "narrative": "The payers keep confirming demand and keep getting punished for funding it. Google Cloud backlog reached $514bn, up more than $50bn sequentially, with just over half expected to convert to revenue inside 24 months and cloud revenue up 82% to $24.8bn. Amazon raised capex to $220bn and said explicitly that even that will not meet 2026 demand, with 2027 the same. Meta narrowed full-year capex upward to $130-145bn.\n\nThe combined 2026 figure across the four now runs to roughly $720-745bn, and this is the cleanest demand signal on the map. But two qualifications belong on it. Part of the raises is memory cost inflation rather than incremental units, so the dollars overstate the pull on optics. And none of these companies disclose accelerator mix or capacity, so every read-through to a named supplier is inference.\n\nThe cash cost is now visible. Meta's free cash flow fell to $784m against $31.1bn of quarterly capex. Alphabet is the one whose spend is funded by an accelerating business, which is why it is the desk's accumulate leg here.",
