        "ticker": "MU",
        "kind": "exit-note",
        "title": "Leaving at the margin peak",
        "dek": "Drops out of Core on score at rank 21. Record DRAM revenue at 84.9% gross margin is a cycle peak, and only a fifth of volume is contracted.",
        "publishedAt": "2026-08-03T00:00:00Z",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-03T00:00:00Z",
        "rating": "watch",
        "sections": [
          {
            "heading": "Snapshot",
            "body": "$MU leaves the verdict roster this week on score, ranking 21st. Its last recorded desk stance was watch; [[sndk]] carried the [[memory]] exposure throughout."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Thesis",
            "body": "AI demand structurally repriced memory, and long-term supply agreements convert a notoriously cyclical business into something closer to a contracted one."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Why own it",
            "body": "Fiscal Q3 DRAM revenue was a record $31.3bn, up 343% year on year at 84.9% consolidated gross margin, with 16 strategic customer agreements and remaining performance obligations above $5bn. The demand lock is contractual rather than anecdotal."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Risks",
            "body": "Only about 20% of DRAM and 30% of NAND volume is covered by those agreements, so roughly four fifths of DRAM volume remains exposed to spot pricing. The contracts smooth the cycle rather than remove it.\n\nAn 84.9% gross margin is a cycle peak, not a run-rate, and the agreements defend floor prices and volume rather than the pricing above the floor \u2014 which is the part currently carrying the earnings."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Bottom line",
            "body": "Exits coverage on rank, and the timing is not unwelcome. The structural memory case is intact; the entry point at peak margin was not, and [[sndk]] already expresses the upcycle with more torque."
          }
        ],
